================================================================================
CAP-SET GAME on F_3^d  --  Grundy value of the empty cap G(d)
Standalone research probe.  2026-07-04.
================================================================================

GAME (impartial, normal play, last player to move wins):
  Position = a cap C subset F_3^d = a point set with NO 3 distinct collinear
             points (no {a,b,c} subset C with a+b+c=0 in F_3^d).
  Move     = add a point p not in C keeping C a cap (p completes no line with
             two existing points: no a,b in C with a+b+p=0).
  G(d)     = Grundy value of the EMPTY cap.  (G=0  <=>  P-position  <=>  2nd
             player wins.)  Known: G(1)=G(2)=G(3)=0.  Conjecture: G(d)=0 for all d.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  G(1) = 0        (reproduced)
  G(2) = 0        (reproduced)
  G(3) = 0        (reproduced)
  G(4) = 0   <==  NEW.  The "always P (G=0)" conjecture SURVIVES d=4.
                  Second player wins the cap-set game on F_3^4 (81 points).
  G(5) : NOT DETERMINED -- intractable with this approach in the memory/time
         budget (see the d=5 section).

  G(4)=0 was computed TWO independent ways that agree exactly:
    * outcome-only (P/N) search : empty is P  ->  G(4)==0, 8191 classes,  5.7 s
    * full Grundy (mex, no cutoff): G(4) = 0 exactly, 9908 classes, 16.6 s
  and by TWO independent binaries (a u128 build and a 256-bit build) that
  produce byte-identical class counts.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SYMMETRY REDUCTION USED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The full affine group AGL(d,3) = translations (3^d) semidirect GL(d,3) preserves
caps and preserves the game, so it is Grundy-preserving.  We memoise on an
AGL-CANONICAL FORM of each cap.  |GL(4,3)| ~= 24.26 million, so orbiting the full
group per state is out; instead:

  canon(C) = min over origin o in a canonically-chosen subset S(C) of C,
             of the GL-min-image of (C - o),
  where the GL-min-image maps an ordered independent frame drawn from the cap's
  OWN vectors to the standard basis e_0,e_1,... (index 3^j is the smallest new
  index a fresh basis vector can introduce at depth j), found by a prefix-pruned,
  best-first DFS over frames.

  Speed comes from restricting, by an AGL-EQUIVARIANT per-point signature, both
    (a) the origin set S(C) = argmax_o sighash(o), and
    (b) the first frame vector b_0 = argmax over directions of endpoint sighash,
  where sighash(p) = hash( par(p), sorted multiset of (cw{p,q}, par(q)) ),
    par(p)  = # parallelograms through p in C = #{(q,r): q,r in C\{p}, q!=r,
              (q+r-p) in C},
    cw{p,q} = #{e != p,q : (p+q-e) in C}.
  Because each restricting set is equivariant and the per-origin GL-min is exact,
  the result is a PROVABLY COMPLETE invariant (canon(C) is always an AGL-image of
  C, so it can never merge two distinct orbits).  It is NOT the global lex-min
  image, so it is validated differently (below).

  This restriction cut canon DFS nodes ~25-85x on the mid-size caps that dominate
  the search (size-10: 5494 -> 205 nodes; size-15: 26966 -> 245 nodes), turning a
  solver that produced ZERO progress on d=4 in 120 s into one that finishes d=4
  in seconds.

A translation-only quotient (min over the 3^d translations; O(n*k), ~1 us) was
also implemented and is exact/Grundy-preserving, but it is FAR too coarse: the
d=4 outcome search under translation-only passed 32 MILLION classes and was still
growing linearly (every cap in AG(4,3) is reachable, and there are billions), so
it cannot finish in memory.  The full AGL quotient is ~165x smaller at d=3 and
proved decisive at d=4 (9908 classes total).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VALIDATION (why G(4)=0 is trustworthy)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. G(1)=G(2)=G(3)=0 reproduced (both outcome and full Grundy).
  2. Canon completeness vs BRUTE full-group AGL for d<=3:
       - invariance: canon(C) == canon(g.C) for random g in AGL  -> 0 failures
       - orbit-partition match: canon(.) and brute canon_brute(.) induce the
         IDENTICAL partition on the random sample
       d=2: invariance_fail=0 partition_fail=0 / 300000 samples (5 classes)
       d=3: invariance_fail=0 partition_fail=0 /  60000 samples (17 classes)
  3. Canon equivariance at d=4 under 200000 random affine maps -> 0 failures.
     (Completeness at d=4 needs no brute enumeration: canon(C) is by construction
      an AGL-image of C, so distinct orbits cannot collide; only equivariance is
      an implementation risk, and that is what the invariance test checks.)
  4. The 256-bit build reproduces the u128 build EXACTLY: G(1..4) and every memo
     count (3, 5, 17, 8191 outcome, 9908 full) match to the digit.
  5. Canon equivariance at d=5 under 20000 random affine maps -> 0 failures.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEMO SIZE / WALL TIME / MEMORY  (single-threaded)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  d   mode      G   classes (memo)   wall     peak RSS
  1   full      0   3                0.000s   <5 MB
  2   full      0   5                0.000s   <5 MB
  3   full      0   17               0.006s   <5 MB
  4   outcome   0   8191             5.7 s    <100 MB
  4   full      0   9908             16.6 s   <100 MB   (= # AGL(4,3)-inequivalent
                                                          caps of ALL sizes 0..20,
                                                          all reachable from empty)
  Memory was capped (env CAP_MAX_SLOTS_POW2, default 2^28 slots ~ 4.6 GB) but the
  d=4 memo (9908 entries) is tiny, so the cap never engaged.  Nothing else on the
  box was disrupted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
d = 5  (243 points; conditional stretch -- DID NOT FINISH)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
F_3^5 needs a >128-bit set, so a separate 256-bit build (capset5) was written and
cross-validated to reproduce d<=4 exactly.  The d=5 outcome search then:
  * ran ~6 min at 100% CPU and had evaluated < 1,000,000 nodes with a tiny memo
    (~10 MB) -- i.e. it never got near the class-count wall; it was stuck
    canonicalising the deep large caps.
  * The DFS dives immediately to a maximal cap (max cap in F_3^5 = 45 points), and
    canonicalising large dim-5 caps is very expensive: on near-maximal, highly
    symmetric caps the per-point signature loses discrimination, so the frame DFS
    falls back toward exhaustive (the same effect seen at d=4 only for the rare
    size-20 complete cap, at ~39 ms each).
  * Canon cost at d=5 is ~5-10x the d=4 cost even at mid sizes (size 12-16 ~500-670
    us vs ~75 us at d=4), and the total AGL-class count for d=5 is far larger than
    d=4's 9908 (d=3 -> d=4 grew ~583x).
  Largest depth reached: full (max cap ~45; DFS was deep in the first branch).
  Best partial evidence for d=5: NONE beyond the validated canon -- the search did
  not complete even the first maximal branch, so no statement about G(5) is made.
  Making d=5 tractable would need a genuinely faster canonical LABELLING (nauty-
  style iterative refinement to a discrete colouring, branching only over real
  automorphisms) rather than the "restrict + exhaustive-tail" scheme here.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FILES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  capset.rs   : prior agent's brute reference (correct but does not finish d=4;
                canon ~2-22 ms/cap).  Kept for the rules only.
  capset2.rs  : u128 solver (d<=4).  Fast restricted-AGL canon.  <-- main d=4 result
  capset5.rs  : 256-bit port (d<=5).  Cross-validated identical to capset2 for d<=4;
                used for the d=5 attempt.
  results.log : this file.

  Build: rustc -C opt-level=3 -C target-cpu=native -C link-arg=-fuse-ld=mold \
                capset2.rs -o capset2      (likewise capset5.rs)
  Run:   ./capset2 <d> <mode> [sym]   mode = outcome|solve|validate|invcheck|bench
                                      sym  = agl (default) | trans
         env CAP_MAX_SLOTS_POW2=<k>   caps memo at 2^k slots
         env CAP_INVCHECK_ITERS=<n>   invcheck sample count

  Reproduce the headline:
    ./capset2 2 validate          # 0/0 fails, 5 classes
    ./capset2 3 validate          # 0/0 fails, 17 classes
    ./capset2 4 invcheck          # 0 fails / 200000 affine maps
    ./capset2 4 solve  agl        # G(4) = 0, memo 9908
    ./capset2 4 outcome agl       # empty is P => G(4)==0, memo 8191
